Understands extended speeches, lectures, articles and reports of the professional, academic, or daily life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "good-natured", "type": "name"}, {"content": "pleasant; friendly", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They are calm, good-natured and are often good delegators. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you use when talking about people oriented roles. ", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "confrontation", "type": "name"}, {"content": "an animated clash or disagreement", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They dislike confrontation.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud08", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "stable", "type": "name"}, {"content": "sensible;  reasonable; dependable", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Resource investigators can be described as stable, dominant and extroverted.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "prone", "type": "name"}, {"content": "likely to do something ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They are prone to dismiss ideas as quickly as they bring them up.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "improviser", "type": "name"}, {"content": "someone who can do something without any preparation", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They like pressure situations and are good improvisers.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "personal responsibility", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the idea that people cause an \u200bevent or \u200bsituation to \u200bhappen by their own actions", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: They may delegate too much, greatly reducing their personal responsibility.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to demolish", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to prove that an idea is completely wrong", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: If someone produces an idea, their instinct is to build upon it, rather than demolish it.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud07", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "dominance", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the idea of being more powerful, more important, or more noticeable than other people", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Team workers can be described as stable, extroverted and low in dominance.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "uncompetitiveness", "type": "name"}, {"content": " the \u200bability to not \u200bcompete \u200bsuccessfully", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Yet, to some, their uncompetitiveness and dislike for conflict may make them seem weak.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn11le06ob06aud09", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Reason Clauses Using <span class=\"italic\">in that</span>, <span class=\"italic\">insofar as</span>, <span class=\"italic\">inasmuch as</span>, <span class=\"italic\">for</span>", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "When you want to provide a reason of why something happens, you can use <span class=\"strong\">reason clauses</span> beginning with: <span class=\"strong\">  in that</span>, <span class=\"strong\"> insofar as</span> and <span class=\"strong\"> inasmuch as</span>.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">in that</span>, <span class=\"strong\"> insofar as</span> and <span class=\"strong\">inasmuch as </span>to justify what you have just said by indicating that the statement is true. You use these words in fairly formal English.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Examples:   <br><span class=\"italic\">He's quite difficult to work with, <span class=\"strong\"> in that </span>he is always complaining about the job. </span><br/> In this case, <span class=\"strong\"> in that</span> means \"because\".<br/><br/>  <span class=\"italic\">We are expecting a busy week <span class=\"strong\"> insofar as</span> all the appointments have been booked. </span><br/> In this case, <span class=\"strong\"> insofar as</span> means \"as far as\".<br/> <span class=\"italic\">The product is guaranteed <span class=\"strong\"> inasmuch as</span> we will replace it within thirty days if there are any problems. </span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">In this case, <span class=\"strong\"> inasmuch as </span> means \"considering that\".</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">For</span><br/> You use<span class=\"strong\"> for</span> as a word that may replace \"seeing that\", \"since\" or \"because\". You use <span class=\"strong\">for</span> in very formal or literary contexts only. <br/> Example: <br/>  <span class=\"italic\">I was very nervous <span class=\"strong\">for</span> it was my first day. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Coordinators are traditional team leaders <span class=\"strong\">in that</span> they lead the team to what they understand to be the team's goals and objectives. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Team workers don't like personal confrontation <span class=\"strong\">insofar as</span> they often try to evade personal disputes whenever possible. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "They are the most social and communicative members of the team <span class=\"strong\">inasmuch as </span>they know a great deal about the personal lives of their fellow team members. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "They are excellent listeners <span class=\"strong\">for </span> they are able to understand each team member's value.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Report Verbs With That-Clauses", "type": "heading"}, {"content": " You use <span class=\"strong\">that-clauses</span> to talk about facts or beliefs. They are common after report verbs and related nouns.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "For example:<br><span class=\"italic\">She explained <span class=\"strong\">that the insurance policy was about to change</span>.</span>  <br/>  <span class=\"italic\">Her explanation was <span class=\"strong\">that the insurance policy was about to change</span>.</span> ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "After some report verbs, you use the subjunctive (infinitive without <span class=\"italic\">to</span>) in the that-clause.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "For example:<br><span class=\"italic\">I suggested that she <span class=\"strong\">remove </span>any reference to the scandal in the lessons.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "It's noticed that they are often extremely likeble upon first meeting the team.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "They try to help and feel that others should do the same. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "She explained that a good manager makes all the difference to a team.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Work with a partner.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Develop a survey to identify the characteristics of a people-oriented person.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Make 3 questions for each of the three roles: coordinator, team-worker, resource investigator.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Interview your classmates and take notes of their responses.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Next, tabulate your results with a partner.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Explain the different characteristics of each people-oriented role.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Reveal your findings to the class based on your classmates' answers.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Also, reveal which category you would belong to and explain why.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I collaborate with a partner.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I develop a survey to help identify people-oriented roles for my classmates.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I create questions to interview my classmates with.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I tabulate our results and reveal them.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I reveal which category I belong to and explain why.", "type": "text"}]}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "WLC", "img_path": "", "questions": [], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can discuss reasons and results of an event or action related to work and jobs.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can talk or report about things, actions, events, or people in a work environment.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about team roles.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What skills does a people-oriented person have?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " Are you a people-oriented person? Why or why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"con